#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# finite_field_embedding

#% text
# `finite_field_embedding(K, F)` gives an embedding of the finite field `K` into the finite field `F`.

#% text
# `finite_field_embedding(a -> b)` gives the embedding that maps the element `a`, a generator of its field, to `b`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `E(c)` gives the image of `c` under the embedding `E`; `E("Projection")(d)` gives an element of `K` mapped to `d` when `d` is in the image.

#% item
# An embedding of `F_(p^m)` into `F_(p^n)` exists exactly when the characteristics agree and `m` divides `n`; otherwise the call stays unevaluated.

#% item
# The embedding is shown as `finite_field_embedding(x -> y)`: the generator `x` of `K` and its image `y`, a root of the defining polynomial of `K` in `F`.

#% item
# When both fields have Conway defining polynomials the image is `x^((p^n - 1)/(p^m - 1))`: these embeddings are compatible, embedding through an intermediate field gives the same map. Otherwise the root with the least index is taken.

#% item
# The projection is linear over the prime field and inverts the embedding on its image; outside the image it is not multiplicative.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
K = finite_field(3, 2)

#% cell
F = finite_field(3, 4)

#% cell
e = finite_field_embedding(K, F)

#% cell
e
#% output
#= finite_field_embedding(finite_field_element(finite_field(3, &(2 + 2*&1 + &1^2), "Polynomial"), [0, 1]) -> finite_field_element(finite_field(3, &(2 + 2*&1^3 + &1^4), "Polynomial"), [1, 0, 2, 2]))

#% cell
y = e(K(5))

#% cell
e("Projection")(y)
#% output
#= finite_field_element(finite_field(3, &(2 + 2*&1 + &1^2), "Polynomial"), [2, 1])

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# An embedding fixed by the image of another generator of `K`:

#% cell
g = K([1, 1])

#% cell
r = finite_field_embedding(g -> e(g))

#% text
# A prime field embeds into each of its extensions:

#% cell
finite_field_embedding(finite_field(3, 1), F)(2)
#% output
#= finite_field_element(finite_field(3, &(2 + 2*&1^3 + &1^4), "Polynomial"), [2])

#% text
# Fields of different characteristics have no embedding:

#% cell
finite_field_embedding(finite_field(2, 2), finite_field(3, 2))
#% output
#= finite_field_embedding(finite_field(2, &(1 + &1 + &1^2), "Polynomial"), finite_field(3, &(2 + 2*&1 + &1^2), "Polynomial"))

#% text
# Fields in the exponential representation, and an embedding with the image of the generator given:

#% cell
[finite_field_embedding(finite_field(3, 2, "Exponential"), finite_field(3, 4, "Exponential"))(finite_field(3, 2, "Exponential")(5)), finite_field_embedding(finite_field(2, 2, "Exponential"), finite_field(2, 4, "Exponential"))]
#% output
#= [finite_field_element(finite_field(3, &(2 + 2*&1^3 + &1^4), "Exponential"), 41), finite_field_embedding(finite_field_element(finite_field(2, &(1 + &1 + &1^2), "Exponential"), 2) -> finite_field_element(finite_field(2, &(1 + &1 + &1^4), "Exponential"), 6))]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The embedding respects sums and products:

#% cell
u = K(4)

#% cell
v = K(7)

#% cell
[e(u + v) == e(u) + e(v), e(u * v) == e(u) * e(v)]
#% output
#= [true, true]

#% text
# Embeddings between Conway fields compose:

#% cell
L = finite_field(3, 8)

#% cell
finite_field_embedding(F, L)(e(u)) == finite_field_embedding(K, L)(u)
#% output
#= true

#% text
# Another root of the defining polynomial gives another embedding, the one composed with the Frobenius map; both are homomorphisms:

#% cell
K2 = finite_field(3, 2)
F6 = finite_field(3, 6)
e2 = finite_field_embedding(K2, F6)
t = finite_field_embedding(K2([0, 1]) -> e2(K2([0, 1]))^3)
[map(k => t(K2(k)) == e2(K2(k))^3, range(0, 8)), map(k => e2("Projection")(e2(K2(k))) == K2(k), range(0, 8))]
#% output
#= [[true, true, true, true, true, true, true, true, true], [true, true, true, true, true, true, true, true, true]]

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# There is no embedding unless the degree of `K` divides that of `F`, and an embedding takes only elements of its source field; both stay unevaluated:

#% cell
[finite_field_embedding(finite_field(3, 4), K2), finite_field_embedding(finite_field(3, 4), F6), e2(7), e2(F6(3))]
#% output
#= [finite_field_embedding(finite_field(3, &(2 + 2*&1^3 + &1^4), "Polynomial"), finite_field(3, &(2 + 2*&1 + &1^2), "Polynomial")), finite_field_embedding(finite_field(3, &(2 + 2*&1^3 + &1^4), "Polynomial"), finite_field(3, &(2 + 2*&1 + &1^2 + 2*&1^4 + &1^6), "Polynomial")), finite_field_element(finite_field(3, &(2 + 2*&1 + &1^2 + 2*&1^4 + &1^6), "Polynomial"), [1]), finite_field_embedding(finite_field_element(finite_field(3, &(2 + 2*&1 + &1^2), "Polynomial"), [0, 1]) -> finite_field_element(finite_field(3, &(2 + 2*&1 + &1^2 + 2*&1^4 + &1^6), "Polynomial"), [2, 2, 1, 2, 0, 2]))(finite_field_element(finite_field(3, &(2 + 2*&1 + &1^2 + 2*&1^4 + &1^6), "Polynomial"), [0, 1]))]

#% text
# A generator mapped to an element that is no root of the defining polynomial makes an embedding that cannot be applied:

#% cell
finite_field_embedding(K2([0, 1]) -> F6([0, 1]))(K2(2))
#% output
#= failure(argument, finite_field_embedding, "the image is not a root of the minimal polynomial of the generator")(finite_field_element(finite_field(3, &(2 + 2*&1 + &1^2), "Polynomial"), [2]))

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `finite_field`, `finite_field_element`, `conway_polynomial`, `frobenius_automorphism`
